{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>These are the generations of Noah.</b> Ibn Ezra and Radak explained its meaning as his deeds/events, like the sense of \"What the day will bear\" (Proverbs 27:1), and it alludes to the entire passage. But this is not correct in my eyes, for a person's deeds are not his \"generations\" (תולדות). The correct interpretation is the literal meaning, like \"These are the generations of the sons of Noah\" (Genesis 10:1), \"And these are the generations of Ishmael\" (Genesis 25:12) — it should say \"These are the generations of Noah: Shem, Ham, and Japheth,\" but it reverted to \"and Noah begot\" because it interrupted and said \"Noah was a righteous man, blameless,\" to inform why He commanded him regarding the ark. And even though it already said above (Genesis 5:32), \"And Noah was five hundred years old, and Noah begot Shem, Ham, and Japheth,\" it mentions them once more, to tell that he was not like all his forefathers who would bear daughters and sons. And this is the reason for \"three sons,\" for it mentions their number, to say that these three alone were his generations and were saved by his merit, and from these the whole earth was spread out." "<b>A righteous man, blameless he was.</b> Scripture mentions that he was innocent/zakai and complete in his righteousness, to inform that he was worthy of being saved from the flood, for he had no punishment at all since he was blameless in justice/zedek. For the righteous man (צדיק) is the one acquitted in judgment, the opposite of the wicked, as it says, \"and they shall acquit the righteous and condemn the wicked\" (Deuteronomy 25:1), and likewise, \"and You are righteous in all that has come upon us, for You have dealt truthfully\" (Nehemiah 9:33), and likewise, \"In righteousness/zzedek you shall judge your fellow\" (Leviticus 19:15). But regarding Abraham, where it says (Genesis 18:19), \"to do righteousness and justice,\" it praised him with zedek, which is justice, and with mercy/rahamim, which is zzedakah. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (on Genesis 6:9): righteous in his deeds, blameless in his heart, and it is written (Ezekiel 28:15), \"You were blameless in your ways.\" And after it says that he was a righteous man — for he was not a man of violence and did not corrupt his way like the sons of his generation who were guilty — it says that he walked with the revered Name, to fear Him alone, that he was not led astray after those who divine the heavens, after soothsayers and cloud-readers, and all the more so after idolatry, and he did not listen to them at all, but with the Name alone he constantly cleaved and walked in the way that HaShem chose or that He would instruct him, for he was a prophet. And this is like the sense of \"After YHWH your Elohim you shall walk, and Him you shall fear\" (Deuteronomy 13:5), which is stated regarding the distancing of the prophet who prophesies to worship idolatry and gives a sign or wonder, as I shall explain, and I shall further mention this in the verse \"Walk before Me and be blameless\" (Genesis 17:1) — if he is blameless in his opinions with Me. Now, since Noah was righteous and not liable to punishment, his sons and his household were also worthy of being saved by his merit, for it would have been a punishment upon him if his seed were cut off. Or one may say that he was a complete righteous man and his sons and his household were also righteous, for he taught them, as it is written (Genesis 18:19), \"For I have known him, to the end that he may command his children and his household.\"" "<b>In his generations.</b> There are among our Sages those who interpreted it for the good — all the more so if he had been in a generation of righteous ones. And there are those who interpret it for disparagement, in the language of Rashi. But the correct interpretation in my eyes according to the peshat is that its meaning is to say that he alone was the righteous one in those generations — there was no righteous or blameless person among his generations besides him. And likewise, \"for you I have seen as righteous before Me in this generation\" (Genesis 7:1) — there is no one else in the generation worthy of being saved. And it says \"in his generations\" because many generations had passed from the time they corrupted themselves, and there was no righteous person on earth besides him. And let not the matter of our Sages concerning Methuselah be difficult for you, for Scripture recounts only that there was no righteous person worthy of being saved from the flood among all those generations."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:25:49.760181394Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}